BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Adult Afternoon Movie - The Choral (2025)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261103T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261103T160000
UID:6a6125f2e9de6536001d92a8@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:1916. As war rages on the Western Front\, the choral society o
 f Ramsden\, Yorkshire has lost most of its men to the army. The Choral's a
 mbitious committee\, determined to press ahead\, decides to recruit local 
 young males to swell their ranks. They must also engage a new chorus maste
 r\, and despite their suspicions that he has something to hide\, their bes
 t bet seems to be Dr. Henry Guthrie (Ralph Fiennes) driven\, uncompromisin
 g\, and recently returned from a career in Germany. As conscription papers
  start to arrive\, the whole community discovers that the best response to
  the chaos that is laying waste to their lives is to make music together.\
 nR\, 113 minutes\n**Subtitles for the Deaf and Hard of Hearing (SDH) when 
 available.
LOCATION:Riverdale\, Program Room 1
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a6125f2e9de6536001d92a8
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Excel Basics 3: PivotTables & Conditional Formatting
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261207T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261207T153000
UID:6abbe679ebad22004541c408@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Build on your basic Microsoft Excel skills and learn to use Ex
 cel data effectively.\nIn this third session\, you will:\nUse Conditional 
 Formatting to visualize data\nExplore the power of PivotTables for data su
 mmary and analysis\nSort and filter the data in tables and using slicers\n
 Take home practice data to improve your new skills\nUpon completion of thi
 s class\, you will be able to get more from your data using Pivot tables.\
 nPrerequisites:\nExcel Basics 1 and 2 or equivalent knowledge. Basic mouse
  and keyboard skills required.\nFormat: Hands-on with demonstrations\nInfo
 rmation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is colle
 cted by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sectio
 ns 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event 
 and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how th
 e library handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\
 nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G
 8.\nAccessibility\nLive or automated closed captioning\, sign language int
 erpretation\, intervenor services\, or attendant services are available up
 on request. Please submit your request at least 2 weeks in advance to:\ntp
 l.ca/accessibility-events\n.
LOCATION:Riverdale\, Program Room 1
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6abbe679ebad22004541c408
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Low Income Retirement Planning
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261015T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261015T160000
UID:6a8c4fe960ccaf01c02085db@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Learn about government benefits\, tax credits\, and strategies
  to help protect your retirement income. Presented by John Stapleton from 
 Open Policy Ontario.\nThese sessions are designed for seniors with low inc
 ome and those who advise them.\nRegistration not required. Drop-in welcome
 .\nThursday Oct 1: What Do Seniors Get?\nLearn about benefits for older a
 dults living in Ontario\, including Canada Pension Plan (CPP)\, Old Age Se
 curity (OAS)\, Guaranteed Income Supplement (GIS)\, and Guaranteed Annual 
 Income Supplement (GAINS).\nThursday Oct 15: Understanding the Personal In
 come Tax System.\nWhat is a taxable income and how can tax credits\, deduc
 tions\, entitlements and exemptions help to reduce taxable income.\nThursd
 ay Oct 29: Protecting Your Retirement Income.\nLearn how to reduce claw b
 acks to GIS and GAINS payments if receiving additional income from employm
 ent\, a private pension\, or a Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP).\
 nThursday Nov 12: Coming to Canada and Leaving Canada.\nLearn about eligib
 le benefits for those that resided in Canada for fewer than 40 years betw
 een the ages of 18 and 65\, or for those planning on emigrating from Canad
 a after retiring.\nThursday Nov 26: Tax Credits and Benefits for Seniors a
 nd People with Disabilities.\nLearn about other tax credits and benefits f
 or seniors\, people with disabilities\, and caregivers\, including the dis
 ability tax credit\, the Canada caregiver credit\, and the home accessibil
 ity tax credit.
LOCATION:Queen/Saulter
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a8c4fe960ccaf01c02085db
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Low Income Retirement Planning
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261126T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261126T160000
UID:6a8c4fe960ccaf01c02085de@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:November is Financial Literacy Month.\nLearn about government
  benefits\, tax credits\, and strategies to help protect your retirement i
 ncome. Presented by John Stapleton from Open Policy Ontario.\nThese sessio
 ns are designed for seniors with low income and those who advise them.\nRe
 gistration not required. Drop-in welcome.\nThursday Oct 1: What Do Senior
 s Get?\nLearn about benefits for older adults living in Ontario\, includin
 g Canada Pension Plan (CPP)\, Old Age Security (OAS)\, Guaranteed Income S
 upplement (GIS)\, and Guaranteed Annual Income Supplement (GAINS).\nThursd
 ay Oct 15: Understanding the Personal Income Tax System.\nWhat is a taxabl
 e income and how can tax credits\, deductions\, entitlements and exemption
 s help to reduce taxable income.\nThursday Oct 29: Protecting Your Retirem
 ent Income.\nLearn how to reduce claw backs to GIS and GAINS payments if 
 receiving additional income from employment\, a private pension\, or a Reg
 istered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP).\nThursday Nov 12: Coming to Canada
  and Leaving Canada.\nLearn about eligible benefits for those that reside
 d in Canada for fewer than 40 years between the ages of 18 and 65\, or for
  those planning on emigrating from Canada after retiring.\nThursday Nov 26
 : Tax Credits and Benefits for Seniors and People with Disabilities.\nLear
 n about other tax credits and benefits for seniors\, people with disabilit
 ies\, and caregivers\, including the disability tax credit\, the Canada ca
 regiver credit\, and the home accessibility tax credit.
LOCATION:Queen/Saulter
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a8c4fe960ccaf01c02085de
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
