BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Indigenous Medicine Workshop: Cedar Salve
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261030T103000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261030T113000
UID:6abaae42901b02002cbe83f2@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:In this workshop\, participants will learn about the significa
 nce of Cedar from an Indigenous perspective\, including its use as a t
 raditional medicine and its beneficial properties. Attendees will also mak
 e a cedar salve to take home! All supplies will be provided.\nThe program
  will be capped at the first 15 participants.\nTanya Gluvakov is a mixed M
 ohawk woman from Toronto with family and "roots" from Six Nations of the G
 rand River in Oshweken\, Ontario. The founder of Roots and Raven\, Tanya i
 s a herbalist\, a mom\, a teacher\, and auntie\, who teaches several works
 hops about medicines from the land and how connecting with them helps us f
 ind our way. She also leads several projects that bring the resurgence of 
 medicines and accessibility to urban communities\nInformation Collection N
 otice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto 
 Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, a
 nd 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program registra
 tion\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles yo
 ur information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n
 \, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Jane/Sheppard\, Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6abaae42901b02002cbe83f2
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:ACT-ively Preventing Elder Abuse
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261116T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261116T160000
UID:6aaea5c3f5c6780c7049389e@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Break down the stigma around elder abuse and learn how to reco
 gnize and respond to it in a safe way. Join a facilitator from Theatre of 
 the Beat for this practical and engaging workshop where you'll learn to:\n
 · identify the different types of elder abuse\n· Identify the signs of e
 lder abuse\n· Using theatre exercises\, develop strategies to be an 'acti
 ve bystander' in situations where elder abuse might be present. No theatre
  experience required\, but participation in role plays is encouraged!\nAbo
 ut Theatre of the Beat:\nTheatre of the Beat (TOTB) is a Canadian touring 
 theatre company working to catalyze conversations on social justice and it
 s intersection with the beliefs of the communities in which we find oursel
 ves.\nLight refreshments are provided for all participants.\nRegistration 
 is encouraged.  Please register online.\nInformation Collection Notice: T
 he personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Li
 brary under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d).
  The information will only be used for event and program registration\, de
 livery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your inform
 ation can be directed to Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5
 981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Don Mills\, Auditorium
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6aaea5c3f5c6780c7049389e
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
